WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Defensive data access uses parameterized patterns so data values are not treated as query syntax.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:15.880
Whether the application uses Entity Framework, Dapper, ADO.NET, or another data layer, query construction should keep structure and values separate.

3
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.660
This is a baseline habit, not an optional hardening step.

4
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.620
ORMs help, but they do not remove design responsibility.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:37.960
Developers still need to review query shape, tenant filters, object-level authorization, eager loading, lazy loading, performance limits, and whether the returned data is appropriate for the current user and operation.

6
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:43.340
A safe query is not only syntactically safe; it is also authorized and bounded.

7
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:52.660
Resources such as database connections, files, streams, readers, writers, HTTP responses, and handles should be managed correctly.

8
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.760
Use language and framework patterns that dispose resources when work is complete.

9
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.940
Resource leaks can become availability problems, especially under load or repeated failure conditions.

10
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:08.900
Timeouts, cancellation, size limits, and bounded reads are defensive controls.

11
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:20.280
Long-running queries, unbounded file reads, uncontrolled loops, large uploads, and missing cancellation paths can reduce reliability and make failure harder to control.

12
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.780
Respect cancellation tokens when the caller or framework provides them.

13
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.980
File handling needs careful path and metadata decisions.

14
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.680
User-supplied names should not become trusted storage paths.

15
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:40.780
Validate extensions, content type expectations, size limits, storage location, and authorization.

16
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:48.000
Keep uploaded or imported content away from executable paths and separate display names from internal storage identifiers.

17
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.520
The common theme is resource intent.

18
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:59.020
Data and files should flow through known boundaries, be validated, be authorized, be processed with limits, and be cleaned up.

19
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:06.800
Defensive code makes those boundaries clear enough that reviewers can see what happens when input is large, slow, malformed, or unauthorized.
